#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# function_injective

#% text
# `function_injective(f, x)` gives `true` when `f` takes no value twice on its domain and `false` when it does.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A function whose derivative has one sign on its domain is injective; two equal values found among samples of the domain prove that it is not.

#% item
# `function_injective([f, cond], x)` restricts the domain.

#% item
# When neither is shown the call stays unevaluated.

#% item
# Samples that are equal in machine arithmetic for a function that saturates (`logistic_sigmoid` at 40) are compared again at 40 digits.

#% item
# Maps of several variables or components, and polynomials with parameters, are decided by the cylindrical decomposition (`f(x1) == f(x2)` implies `x1 == x2`); a scalar polynomial with parameters is injective where its derivative has one strict sign. Over the complexes a function with a period is not injective.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The exponential:

#% cell
function_injective(e^x, x)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# A cubic with a local maximum and minimum:

#% cell
function_injective(x^3 - x, x)
#% output
#= false

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# The same cubic beyond its minimum:

#% cell
function_injective([x^3 - x, x > 1], x)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# A parabola:

#% cell
function_injective(x^2, x)
#% output
#= false

#% cell
function_injective([x^2, x >= 0], x)
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# A curve in the plane:

#% cell
[function_injective([x^2 + 2*x, x^3 - x], x), function_injective([x^2 + x, x^3 - x], x)]
#% output
#= [true, false]

#% text
# A parameter:

#% cell
function_injective(x^3 + a*x + b, x)
#% output
#= conditional_expression(true, element(a | b, reals) and a > 0)

#% text
# Over the complexes:

#% cell
[function_injective(exp(x), x, complexes), function_injective(log(x), x, complexes), function_injective([x^2, re(x) >= 0, im_part(y) != 0], x, y, complexes)]
#% output
#= [false, true, true]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# A function with a derivative of one sign is monotonic:

#% cell
function_monotonicity(e^x, x)
#% output
#= 1

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A function that is not known stays unevaluated; a step function repeats its values and is not injective:

#% cell
[function_injective(g(x), x), function_injective(floor(x), x)]
#% output
#= [function_injective(g(x), x), false]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `function_monotonicity`, `function_sign`
